1 What bltzread is
******************

bltzread helps you read text in a language you are learning.  It colours
every word by how well you know it, shows a small card with the
dictionary entry when you rest on a word, and remembers your progress.
When a word becomes fully known it can be added to your bltzr skip
lists, so bltzr stops listing it as new vocabulary.

   bltzread never changes the text.  It draws colours on top of it, and
the buffer is read-only while the mode is on.  It works in any buffer: a
plain text file, a web page in EWW, a book opened with nov.el, or the
subtitles of subtle.

   You need Emacs 29.1 or newer and bltzr (the ‘bltzr’ command-line
tool, found on your ‘PATH’ or named by ‘bltzread-bltzr-executable’).
Optional: sdcv with StarDict dictionaries for the cards, and the ‘avy’
package for ‘bltzread-avy-unknown’.

* Menu:

* Installing::


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Installing,  Up: What bltzread is

1.1 Installing
==============

Put the folder on your ‘load-path’ and load it on demand:

     (use-package bltzread
       :load-path "/path/to/bltzread"
       :commands (bltzread-mode bltzread-read-text bltzread-review
                  bltzread-manual bltzread-tutorial))

   The Info version of this manual is ‘bltzread.info’; add the folder to
‘Info-additional-directory-list’ to read it with ‘C-h i’.


