2 The five levels
*****************

Every word has a level.  The names and the colours are options (see
_Colours_); these are the defaults.

Level 1, New
     You have just met it.  Strongest colour.
Level 2, Recognized
     You have seen it and half remember it.
Level 3, Familiar
     You usually understand it.
Level 4, Learned
     You nearly always understand it.  Only an underline.
Level 5, Known
     You know it.  No colour.  This is where a word may go onto your
     skip list.
Ignored
     Names, numbers and other words you do not want to learn.  Shown
     dimly and never counted.

   Words on your bltzr skip lists start out as level 5.  Words bltzread
has never seen start at level 1.  The number of levels is fixed: levels
1 to 4 are "learning", level 5 is "known" (the constants
‘bltzread-max-learning-level’ and ‘bltzread-known-level’).  You can
rename and recolour them, not add more.

   A level applies to the whole word: setting 3 on _hiše_ also sets
_hiša_ and _hišo_ (see ‘bltzread-level-scope’).  Clean reads, in
contrast, count only for the form you actually read.  Tokens without a
letter, such as ‘24’, ‘5.26’ or ‘2,81’, are never marked.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Starting and stopping,  Next: Keys,  Prev: The five levels,  Up: Top

3 Starting and stopping
***********************

Run ‘M-x bltzread-mode’ in a buffer.  bltzread uses the language of the
buffer (‘bltzread-language’, often set as a file-local variable), else
‘bltzread-default-language’, else asks bltzr to guess it from the start
of the text, else asks you for a three letter code such as ‘slv’ or
‘pli’.  It asks bltzr about the words and paints them.  Running the
command again turns the mode off and returns the buffer to what it was.

   To read text you have copied from somewhere else run ‘M-x
bltzread-read-text’.  It uses the region, else the clipboard, else asks
you to type the text, and opens it in a reading page with larger type,
wider line spacing and side margins (see _Pasted text_).


