4.1 Single keys and the prefix
==============================

While the buffer is read-only, which it is by default
(‘bltzread-make-read-only’), single keys are bound without a modifier.
They are not bound in a buffer you can edit, so typing is never in the
way.  Put the cursor on a word and press:

‘5’
     Set that level (the digits ‘1’ to ‘5’).
‘+’ and ‘-’
     Raise or lower the level by one, within 1 to 5; they never wrap.
     Give a count to step further.
‘i’
     Ignore the word.  On an ignored word, stop ignoring it and bring
     back its old level.
‘u’
     Undo the last bltzread change even when other edits came in
     between.  Ordinary ‘undo’ and ‘undo-redo’ also take back and
     restore bltzread changes (level, ignore, note, lookup, finished
     session) in order with everything else, although the buffer is
     read-only.
‘c’
     Close the card.  It returns when you move to another word.
‘d’ and ‘D’
     Show the next or previous dictionary entry when a word has several.
‘l’
     Look the word up in the dictionary (also a double click).  A single
     click opens the card even when the cursor was on the word already.
‘N’
     Write yourself a note about the word.  The card shows it.
‘]’ and ‘[’
     Jump to the next or previous word that is still unknown.  A count
     jumps further; a negative count goes the other way.  If fewer
     remain bltzread moves as far as it can and says how far.

   All of these also work after the prefix ‘bltzread-prefix-key’
(default ‘s’), for example ‘s 3’, and the prefix gives the rarer
commands too:

‘s f’
     ‘bltzread-finish-session’, see _Reading sessions_.
‘s k’
     ‘bltzread-mark-range-known’, mark every word in the range you pick
     as known.
‘s r’
     ‘bltzread-review’, the review list.
‘s m’
     ‘bltzread-range-change-method’, change how you pick the range.
‘s <’ and ‘s >’
     Set the start and the end of the range yourself.
‘s a’
     ‘bltzread-annotate’, read the words again and paint them.
‘s L’
     ‘bltzread-set-language’, change the language of the buffer.
‘.’ (also ‘s .’)
     ‘bltzread-popup-at-point’, show the card for the word at point.

   ‘M-x describe-keymap RET bltzread-command-map’ lists them.  ‘M-x
bltzread-avy-unknown’ (needs the avy package) tags every unknown word in
view with a hint; type one to go to that word and open its card.  It has
no key by default.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Changing the keys,  Prev: Single keys and the prefix,  Up: Keys

4.2 Changing the keys
=====================

The keymap is ‘bltzread-mode-map’.  The prefix has its own option,
‘bltzread-prefix-key’ (nil for none); changing it in Customize rebinds
it at once.  To bind further keys use ‘keymap-set’.  To make a key work
only while the buffer is read-only, as the built-in ones do, wrap the
command with ‘bltzread-when-read-only’:

     (keymap-set bltzread-mode-map "x" (bltzread-when-read-only #'bltzread-ignore))
     (keymap-unset bltzread-mode-map "N" t)

   The mouse gestures are options: ‘bltzread-mouse-lookup-key’ (default
a double click) and ‘bltzread-mouse-popup-key’ (default a click).
Either can be nil.  They are applied when set through Customize or
‘setopt’; if you use ‘setq’, run ‘bltzread--install-mouse-keys’
afterwards.

   The keys listed in the card's last row come from the real keymaps
(see _The card_).


