6.1 Short dictionary entries
============================

The card can show only the part of an entry you care about.  bltzread
knows nothing about any particular dictionary: ‘bltzread-dict-filters’
says, per dictionary (the key is its name) or per language (the key is
the code), which pieces to show.  A spec is one of:

   • A regexp string.  Every match in the entry gives one line: the text
     of its first group, or the whole match.
   • A plist ‘(:class "gloss")’ taking the text just after each element
     with that class (a list for several classes).  More keys: ‘:split "
     · "’ cuts each piece into lines, ‘:max 5’ keeps the first five,
     ‘:join ", "’ puts the lines on one line.
   • A function taking the entry text, for anything else.

   Tags and entities are removed and repeated lines dropped.  The
headword and the dictionary name are not repeated.  If a spec finds
nothing the card falls back to the whole entry on purpose.  The lookup
key still shows the whole entry.  Entries are cached with the filter;
run ‘M-x bltzread-dict-clear-cache’ after changing a spec.  The tutorial
walks through writing one.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Reading sessions,  Next: No-colors mode,  Prev: The dictionary,  Up: Top

7 Reading sessions
******************

You do not have to set every level by hand.  bltzread also promotes
words as you read:

  1. You read part of the text.
  2. You tell bltzread which part you read.  This is a "finish": press
     ‘s f’, or answer the question when you close the buffer.
  3. Every word in that part that you did not look up gets one "clean
     read".
  4. When a word has enough clean reads it moves up one level
     (‘bltzread-promotion-thresholds’: how many it needs at each level).
  5. A word you looked up during the session loses its clean reads and
     keeps its level (‘bltzread-lookup-effect’ can also demote it).

   A level you set by hand always wins over this automatic climbing.
‘C-u s f’ finishes and marks everything in the range as known at once.

   When the buffer is closed without a finish, bltzread asks whether to
record your reading (‘bltzread-prompt-on-close’), but only if enough
text was passed (‘bltzread-min-chars-for-prompt’).  A program that
closes buffers can call ‘bltzread-offer-record’ first; it asks once, and
the question is not repeated on close.

* Menu:

* Telling bltzread what you read::


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Telling bltzread what you read,  Up: Reading sessions

7.1 Telling bltzread what you read
==================================

The way is chosen by ‘bltzread-range-method’; you can try them in ‘M-x
bltzread-range-playground’.

farthest
     bltzread proposes everything from where you started to the farthest
     place you reached.  By default that place is the bottom of the text
     on screen.  With ‘bltzread-range-farthest-source’ set to ‘point’ or
     to a function returning a position, it is whatever that returns, so
     only text that was played counts.  ‘bltzread-range-start-function’
     does the same for the start.
points
     You mark a start and an end yourself with ‘s <’ and ‘s >’.
paragraphs
     Paragraph numbers appear in the margin; you type from and to.
lines
     The same, using line numbers.

   After the range is counted, bltzread may move your bookmark to the
end of it (‘bltzread-bookmark-policy’,
‘bltzread-bookmark-name-function’).  ‘bltzread-session-confirm’ says
whether to confirm the range first.

   In nov.el, turning a page replaces the text, so bltzread paints the
new page and forgets the old range.  Finish the session before you turn
the page if you want that reading counted.  Closing the buffer also
asks.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: No-colors mode,  Next: The review list,  Prev: Reading sessions,  Up: Top

8 No-colors mode
****************

Set ‘bltzread-no-colors’ (per buffer with ‘setq-local’, or ‘M-x
bltzread-toggle-colors’) to show no level colours and to disable setting
levels by hand.  The card stays, without the level unless
‘bltzread-no-colors-show-level’ is set.  When you finish or close, the
words you did not look up still get their clean reads and progress as
usual.  To make a "quiet mode" with only the card, see the recipes in
_Extending bltzread_.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: The review list,  Next: Your skip lists,  Prev: No-colors mode,  Up: Top

9 The review list
*****************

Words that reach level 5 are not put on your skip list straight away
unless you ask (‘bltzread-known-write-policy’).  They wait in a queue.
‘M-x bltzread-review’ (or ‘s r’) shows it; its keys are listed in the
header of the list:

‘a’
     Add this word (every word starts as add).
‘d’
     Decide later.  It stays in the queue.
‘r’
     Reject.  The word drops back to ‘bltzread-review-reject-level’
     (default 4).
‘A’
     Mark every word add.
‘x’
     Apply: write the added words to the skip list (after
     ‘bltzread-review-confirm’).

   Words that reached level 5 by reading exposure rather than by hand
are listed first, highlighted with the face ‘bltzread-review-exposure’.
bltzread makes a ‘.bak’ copy of the list file before it writes.  The
hook ‘bltzread-review-after-apply-hook’ runs afterwards.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Your skip lists,  Next: Where bltzread keeps its data,  Prev: The review list,  Up: Top

10 Your skip lists
******************

bltzr has two skip lists: exact words (only the spelling you list) and
word families (a basic word and all its forms).  By default bltzread
adds the exact spelling you read to the exact-words list.  You can
choose word families instead, or be asked each time, and set this per
language (‘bltzread-list-target’, ‘bltzread-list-target-alist’).

   bltzread finds the files in your bltzr settings file
(‘bltzread-bltzr-config-file’, by default ‘~/.config/bltzr/bltzr.toml’).
You can name the files yourself with ‘bltzread-skip-exact-words-files’
and ‘bltzread-skip-word-families-files’.  Until you choose a
‘bltzread-known-write-policy’ (‘review’ or ‘immediate’), bltzread acts
as ‘review’ and warns once.

   The code that writes the words is ‘bltzread-known-writer-function’,
which you can replace.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Where bltzread keeps its data,  Next: Options,  Prev: Your skip lists,  Up: Top

11 Where bltzread keeps its data
********************************

One file per language, in ‘bltzread-state-directory’ (by default a
folder named ‘bltzread’ in your Emacs directory).  It holds levels,
clean-read counts, notes, the queue and a short history.  Files are
plain text and saved a few seconds after a change.  A file that cannot
be read is not overwritten: it is copied to ‘NAME.corrupt’ and you are
warned.


